WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
Branches that fire with their neighbours tend to stay.

2
00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:07.440
The genome stores rules, and the tissue measures the rest.

3
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.640
But measuring needs a working circuit.

4
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.520
Does a brain wait until it is finished to switch on?

5
00:00:17.967 --> 00:00:21.607
An electronic circuit is assembled first, then switched on.

6
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:26.087
Once it is finished, its wiring does not change with use.

7
00:00:27.407 --> 00:00:30.347
A developing neural circuit works while it is being built.

8
00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:33.067
Embryonic neurons fire.

9
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:38.287
Connections made early carry signals while later ones are still growing.

10
00:00:39.487 --> 00:00:43.647
Those signals, and later experience, help decide which connections stay.

11
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.620
The same rule, overproduce then select, works on whole neurons.

12
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.980
Development makes more neurons than the finished circuit keeps, and many of them

13
00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:54.980
die.

14
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:57.653
Which ones live is shaped by competition.

15
00:00:59.233 --> 00:01:04.073
Targets supply limited support, such as nerve growth factor, and the neurons compete

16
00:01:04.073 --> 00:01:05.073
for it.

17
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:06.983
Here is the one idea.

18
00:01:08.203 --> 00:01:10.083
A brain does not wait to be finished.

19
00:01:11.263 --> 00:01:12.703
It runs while it is built.

20
00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:17.963
It makes extra, and what stays is decided after the connections form.

21
00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:23.433
If the human cortex works this way, synapses should rise, then fall.

22
00:01:24.773 --> 00:01:30.553
In 1979, Peter Huttenlocher counted synapses per unit volume in human frontal

23
00:01:30.553 --> 00:01:33.053
cortex, from people of many ages.

24
00:01:34.217 --> 00:01:36.357
Density was already high at birth.

25
00:01:37.617 --> 00:01:42.897
It rose through infancy and peaked at about 1 to 2 years, roughly half again the

26
00:01:42.897 --> 00:01:47.137
adult value. Then it declined through childhood and adolescence.

27
00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:51.290
But the cortex itself keeps growing through childhood.

28
00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:56.590
So does a falling density, on its own, prove that synapses were lost?

29
00:02:02.667 --> 00:02:08.287
Not by itself. A falling density could partly reflect tissue growth.

30
00:02:09.387 --> 00:02:13.927
It fits synapse loss without proving it, and it cannot show what decided which

31
00:02:13.927 --> 00:02:20.147
synapses stayed. Studies in animals that follow the same dendritic spines over time

32
00:02:20.147 --> 00:02:21.587
show the loss directly.

33
00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:25.267
The timing also differs between regions.

34
00:02:26.607 --> 00:02:32.207
In one study, auditory cortex peaked at about 3 months, and prefrontal cortex after

35
00:02:32.207 --> 00:02:38.607
15 months. Another found prefrontal spine loss continuing into the third decade of

36
00:02:38.607 --> 00:02:39.607
life.

37
00:02:39.917 --> 00:02:42.457
Selection is one part of a longer build.

38
00:02:43.737 --> 00:02:47.757
The course follows nine overlapping steps, from induction to refinement.

39
00:02:49.237 --> 00:02:53.997
Selection is most on show in two of them: naturally occurring death, and

40
00:02:53.997 --> 00:02:55.377
activity-dependent refinement.

41
00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:59.870
So a brain is not a board, soldered and then plugged in.

42
00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:05.190
It is a circuit already at work, built with extra, and trimmed as it runs.
